[agent] ## Summary
[agent] **What I did on the platform (all work ran on Databricks via the CLI, nothing local):**
[agent] 1. Created a UC volume `workspace.mlpab32905e.preds_vol` and uploaded the prediction log.
[agent] 2. Logged the predictions into a Delta table `workspace.mlpab32905e.prediction_log` (13,327 rows, Jan 1–Mar 31 2026) with change-data-feed enabled.
[agent] 3. Created a **Lakehouse (Data Quality) Monitor** of type TimeSeries (daily granularity) on the table, producing `prediction_log_profile_metrics` and `prediction_log_drift_metrics`.
[agent] 4. Detected the distribution shift using the warehouse's statistics capability (per-day `avg`/`stddev` aggregation, then hourly drill-down at the boundary).
[agent] **Finding:** The daily mean prediction is stable at ~6.5 (sd ~0.9) through 2026-03-07, then steps up to ~10.2 (sd ~0.9) and stays there for the rest of the log. Hourly resolution confirms the jump happens precisely at 00:00 on 2026-03-08 (last values on 03-07 are ~6; first value on 03-08 is ~10). **Onset: 2026-03-08.**
[agent] **Interface limitation to report:** Databricks Lakehouse Monitoring did *not* work in this workspace. Across **four** successful monitor refreshes (≈10 min each) — including a fresh monitor on a clean CDF-enabled table populated via a row-level `INSERT` — the profiling step consistently wrote **`numOutputRows: 0`** to both the profile and drift metrics tables, even though the source table had 13,327 rows trivially readable via SQL. So the drift tables (`js_distance`, `wasserstein_distance`, `ks_test`, PSI) that would normally pinpoint the onset window were empty and unusable. I fell back to the platform's general SQL/statistics capability (which works) to characterize the distribution and locate the shift. The missing/broken capability is **Lakehouse Monitoring metric computation** — refreshes report SUCCESS but profile zero rows from a non-empty table.
